{#
The "something arrived here" marks — the category/label/role half of the
new-mail marker.

Two macros, one statement, two volumes. Both say that conversations arrived in
here that have never been put in front of you; they differ only in whether they
say how many. The sidebar's dot stays quiet because its rows already carry an
unread badge, and a second bare number one gap along would be two demands with
nothing to tell them apart. The tab pill DOES carry the number, Gmail-style —
"3 new" — and gets away with it because the word is the disambiguator: the bare
grey figure on a tab is unread, the filled pill that says "new" is arrivals,
the same vocabulary split the thread rows use (a "New" pill against an unread
dot). Both live in this file so the two halves of one marker cannot drift when
one of them is restyled.

Rendered at every count and hidden at zero rather than omitted, which is the
idiom every patched marker in this app follows (see the unread_badge macro in
_sidebar.html.twig): the sidebar controller finds it by `data-count-key` after
a Mercure sync and toggles it, and an element that was never rendered cannot be
found.

`bg-accent` rather than a colour of its own: it is the token that follows
whichever of the app's themes is on, so this reads in light and in dark without
a second definition — and the sidebar's label_dot palette is identity colour,
which this is not.

The accessible name is the whole reason `where` is a parameter. A screen reader
meeting a bare dot on "Promotions" gets nothing at all; announcing which place
has new mail is the only form of this marker that carries its meaning.
#}
{% macro new_dot(count, key, where) %}
    {# No margin of its own: every caller already sits in a flex row with a gap,
       and a margin here would be spacing applied twice in the sidebar and
       missing wherever the next caller is not a flex row. The tab strip, which
       is not one, adds its own.

       `flex` and not `inline-block`, and that is not a style preference. Both
       are display utilities, both land in the same Tailwind layer, and the one
       written LATER in the generated stylesheet wins whatever order the class
       attribute is in — `.hidden` is emitted before `.inline-block` and after
       `.flex`. With inline-block the dot rendered at every count and simply
       ignored `hidden`, which is a marker that never goes away. #}
    <span class="new-dot flex w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full shrink-0 bg-accent{{ count > 0 ? '' : ' hidden' }}"
          data-new-dot
          data-count-key="{{ key }}">
        <span class="sr-only">{{ 'sidebar.new_mail_here'|trans({ '%where%': where }) }}</span>
    </span>
{% endmacro %}

{#
The loud variant: a filled pill that says "3 new". The inbox's category tabs
render this one — see the header comment for why they may carry a number when
the sidebar must not.

Same `data-new-dot`, same key namespace, same render-always-hide-at-zero idiom
as the dot above, so every invariant that holds the dots together (the counts
endpoint must emit every rendered key — BadgeSemanticsTest) holds this without
a second rule. What is new is that the patcher writes into it, and the two
`data-*-template` attributes are how: the count arrives over JSON long after
the page's translator is gone, so the pill carries its own translated strings
with the placeholder still in them and the patcher stays string-blind. See
patchNewDots in sidebar_controller.js.

`|trans` without a %count% parameter is deliberate on the template attributes:
Symfony leaves unfilled placeholders in place, which is exactly the form the
patcher needs.

aria-label rather than the dot's sr-only child, following unread_badge in
_sidebar.html.twig — the visible text is a fragment ("3 new" of what?), and a
label naming the place is the announcement that carries the meaning. Keeping
the element childless is also what lets the patcher assign textContent without
knowing what a pill contains.

`tone` is the caller's colour, background and text together, and its default
is the accent pair every other patched marker wears. The category tabs pass
their own — Gmail colours these hints per category, and that identity colour
is the label_dot idiom rather than the theme-token one: a fixed colour that
means "this place", the same in light and dark.

No display utility of its own, unlike the dot — a span is inline already, and
the dot's own comment explains what a display class in the wrong Tailwind
layer order does to `hidden`. The tab's wrapper handles alignment.
#}
{% macro new_pill(count, key, where, tone = null) %}
    <span class="new-pill text-xs font-semibold px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded-full shrink-0 {{ tone ?? 'bg-accent text-accent-ink' }}{{ count > 0 ? '' : ' hidden' }}"
          data-new-dot
          data-count-key="{{ key }}"
          data-count-template="{{ 'tabs.new_count'|trans }}"
          data-label-template="{{ 'sidebar.new_mail_here_count'|trans({ '%where%': where }) }}"
          aria-label="{{ 'sidebar.new_mail_here_count'|trans({ '%count%': count, '%where%': where }) }}"
    >{{ 'tabs.new_count'|trans({ '%count%': count }) }}</span>
{% endmacro %}
